# Public-source and normalization disclosure. The dollar coefficients below are
# not asserted as a single vendor's invoice. They are declared reference inputs
# synthesized from public price structures and official economic studies so all
# governance modes can be evaluated on the same matched cost surface.
MS_COST_BASIS_DISCLOSURE = {
    "method":"Public-source normalized midpoint model. Direct operating-cost coefficients are synthesized from official cloud price structures, U.S. government wage and electricity data, and publicly posted observability and control-plane pricing. The model deliberately avoids selecting the highest advertised tier and is not a vendor quote, contract, invoice, appraisal, or claim that every organization pays the same rate.",
    "public_basis":"Reference categories are drawn from AWS, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Kubernetes service pricing, U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Datadog, Grafana, Uptime Institute, IBM/Ponemon, and CISA public materials recorded in the source registry. Quote-based enterprise licenses are excluded unless the evaluator supplies an actual price.",
    "compute_gpu":"CPU, VM, GPU, and accelerator coefficients are central normalization inputs informed by public AWS, Google Cloud, and Azure pricing structures. They are not copied from a single instance, region, commitment term, or premium high-end configuration.",
    "storage_network":"Storage and network coefficients are central public-price references separated by bare-metal, on-premises, private-cloud, public-cloud, hybrid, and edge environments. Location, egress pattern, commitment, utilization, hardware ownership, and contract discounts can move actual values materially.",
    "kubernetes_control_plane":"The managed Kubernetes control-plane reference uses a publicly posted standard cluster-hour management category. Actual free tiers, enterprise tiers, support plans, discounts, and regional differences are excluded unless supplied by the evaluator.",
    "engineering_labor":"Engineering labor uses the BLS May 2024 software-developer median of $133,080 and a declared 1.45 loaded-cost factor, yielding a $192,966 annual central reference per FTE. The factor represents benefits and employer burden and must be replaced with company payroll and allocation records for a production study.",
    "electricity":"Electricity uses the declared U.S. industrial reference rate from EIA for centralized environments and a separately declared edge assumption. Local tariffs, demand charges, cooling allocation, renewable contracts, and power-usage effectiveness must be substituted for production use.",
    "observability":"Datadog and Grafana public pricing structures inform a normalized observability and control-plane category. Customer-specific retention, ingest, seats, support, negotiated discounts, and bundled services are not inferred.",
    "outage":"Outage and security categories use public studies only as risk anchors. Representative bands are transparent model assumptions, not a claim that a specific company experiences the study average.",
    "security":"The global breach-cost reference is used as a disclosed sensitivity anchor, not as an annual invoice or prediction. Company-specific event frequency, controls, insurance, regulatory exposure, and business interruption must replace it.",
    "sensitivity":"Direct cost categories are reported around the normalized midpoint with a declared +/-10% working sensitivity band. That band expresses model sensitivity, not a statistical guarantee that every company lies within ten percent. Incident-loss economics retain a wider declared uncertainty band because tail losses are materially more variable.",
    "tailoring":"Every cost, license, staffing, utilization, incident, energy, and stack-count input can be replaced upward or downward. The one-stack ledger is the controlling unit; a qualified organization must calibrate it to invoices, contracts, payroll, telemetry, incident records, and equivalent stack count before relying on savings or ROI.",
    "comparison_formula":"For matched run r, environment e, scale n, and governance mode m: additional financial impact versus Kubernetes = C_Kubernetes(r,e,n) - C_m(r,e,n). Positive is additional modeled savings; Kubernetes is zero only because it is compared with itself; negative is additional modeled cost.",
    "calibration_rule":"No modeled dollar result is production truth until a qualified technical and finance team substitutes company-specific values, locks a matched baseline, discloses exclusions and one-time costs, and reconciles the result through a shadow or staged pilot.",
    "retrieval_date":"Public-reference registry reviewed 2026-08-01. Prices and labor data can change; the evaluator must refresh the registry at the time of a commercial study."
}

